I've built and saved the main-A workflow. It starts correctly: one live chat turn answered "Hello?" with the opening line (digital assistant from Freya Bank, call is recorded, "Am I speaking with Ms Lee?"). Beyond that one turn it is untested. As instructed, I didn't run the practice cases, and I didn't do the rule-by-rule recheck against the brief because time ran out.

- **Structure:** the start node is your date-facts code node, unedited. After it come three conversation stages: who's on the line, the security questions, and stating the debt and getting a promise. Between the security questions and the debt there is a silent account-lookup step, and every close goes to a single hang-up node.
- **Code steps:** the account hook is set verbatim on `get_account`. `{{date_facts}}` and the "never compute a date yourself" rule are in all three conversation stages. The debt stage shows each product's own `deadline` and `floor_amount` and tells the model to use them, not work them out. The one rule no code step computes (send a date of birth as `YYYY-MM-DD`, reading a numbers-only date as day, then month, then year) is written into the security-questions stage in the brief's words.
- **Routing:** the call moves on tool results and one extracted variable, not on what the customer seems to mean. The security-questions stage starts only on a clear "it's me". The debt stage starts only when both security answers pass.
- **Failed tools:** every stage tells the model to read `tool_outcome` first and never claim something is recorded after a FAILED result. The promise stage has the brief's rule: try once more, then say it couldn't be recorded and close as `no_promise` / `service_error`, and offer exactly `offer_instead` when a result has it. That rule is in the prompt only; I didn't add the transition that would close the call automatically after two failures.
- **Goodbye:** the model says the goodbye itself and then calls `close_call`; the hang-up node ends the call without speaking. This differs from the routing guide, where the platform speaks the goodbye. I did it because the brief requires thanks and goodbye before `close_call`. The save step also added its own goodbye-to-hang-up wiring, which I haven't checked.

The brief's rules match the date facts, so I made no exceptions.

